"feat(word,word-backend): prove repeated gadgets as instances of one compiled component by tcoratger · Pull Request #2292 · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/pull/2292)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"pattern\":\"opened\"}); Total lines: 572\nL210: Read it in four parts.\nL211: \nL212: Committed cells are exactly the live instances. 64 bits per committed word, 2 words per instance, no padding above the next power of two. Doubling the instances doubles the cells and does nothing else. That is the cost that should scale, and it does, with no per-instance overhead from the component machinery — the assertion in the example is that the two paths agree on this figure exactly.\nL213: Opened values stay at one, and are not a column. The whole protocol ends at one authenticated Boolean-trace opening whatever the instance count, and adding a second call does not add a second one — but that is a structural property of the one point the proving routine passes to the commitment, not something the sweep can vary, so reporting it as a measured column would have been theatre. It is stated in the example's trailing note instead.\nL214: Proof bytes per instance flatten. 1 006 bytes at n=4 down to 52.5 at n=1024, a 19x fall across a 256x range, because the transcript is `O(log n)` — the zerocheck rounds, the two shift sumchecks and the WHIR opening all grow logarithmically.\nL215: Metadata is where the two paths part. The inline path stores 23 wiring entries per instance forever: 98, 190, 374, … 23 558, doubling with every doubling of `n`. The composed path stores 29 entries at every `n`, because the component is compiled once. At n=1024 that is 812x less stored wiring for the same proof.\nL216: The curve that had to flatten is the per-instance one, and both of the per-instance curves flatten: proof bytes per instance falls monotonically, and metadata per instance goes 