## Conversation\nL157: \nL158: ###\nL159: \nL160: 【78†Image: @tcoratger†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【70†tcoratger】 commented 【79†Sep 22, 2026】 •\nL161: \nL162: edited\nL163: \nL164: Loading\nL165: ### Uh oh!\nL166: \nL167: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL168: \nL169: Copy link\nL170: \nL171: Copy Markdown\nL172: ## What\nL173: \nL174: A machine that uses one word gadget many times — a hash round, a bignum limb step, a field operation — used to pay for it by writing the gadget's constraints out once per use. This makes the repeated uses instances of one compiled component instead.\nL175: \nL176: The proof is unchanged. That is the point: the composed path and the written-out path describe one statement and produce a byte-identical proof, so the cost that disappears is the duplicated metadata and nothing that the soundness argument rests on.\nL177: ## The model\nL178: \nL179: A `Component` is a word gadget written against component-local slots. Its body is an ordinary checked `ConstraintSystem`, and its public segment is its interface: the first `public_inputs` slots are inputs, the next `public_outputs` are outputs, and its witness segment is per-instance private storage. A `ComponentCall` pairs a component with a checked instance count. A `Composition` lays several calls out into one statement.\nL277: 【91†feat(sumcheck,binary-pcs,lookup,multilinear-util)!: enforce caller soundness obligations instead of documenting them #2293】\nL278: \nL279: Merged\nL280: \nL281: 【82†Nashtare】 reviewed 【92†Sep 22, 2026】\nL282: \nL283: 【76†View reviewed changes】\nL284: ###\nL285: \nL286: 【93†Image: @Nashtare†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【82†Nashtare】 left a comment\nL287: \nL288: Copy link\nL289: \nL290: Copy Markdown\nL291: \nL292: There was a problem hiding this comment.\nL293: ### Choose a reason for hiding this comment\nL294: \nL295: The reason will be displayed to describe this comment to others. 【94†Learn more†docs.github.com】.\nL296: \nL297: [Input][Input] [Select] Hide\nL298: I merged the head into 【95†`c36bbb7`】 (clean) and ran `cargo check` and `cargo clippy -D warnings` with all targets on `p3-word` and `p3-word-backend`, which are the only crates that use them. Both are clean and the tests pass (33, 65 and 13). The same holds with 【91†#2293】 (【96†`69e6488`】) merged on top. Nothing in this diff builds or drops a `Point` or a claim value, so its new `#[must_use]` attributes don't fire.\nL299: The central claim holds everywhere I looked. With a local probe I compared the composed and lowered forms for these cases: a single instance; a dead call placed first, in the middle, and one whose shift spelling no live call uses; a public-only component; a witness-only component; a component with slots and no relations; 1000 instances of an empty component beside a live one; and a four-call mix at 7, 5, 3 and 9 instances.\nL454: ###\nL455: \nL456: 【93†Image: @Nashtare†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【82†Nashtare】 left a comment\nL457: \nL458: Copy link\nL459: \nL460: Copy Markdown\nL461: \nL462: There was a problem hiding this comment.\nL463: ### Choose a reason for hiding this comment\nL464: \nL465: The reason will be displayed to describe this comment to others. 【94†Learn more†docs.github.com】.\nL466: \nL467: [Input][Input] [Select] Hide\nL468: I went through the new commit. The three earlier commits are unchanged by the rebase (`git range-diff` shows them identical), and nothing under `word/` or `word-backend/` moved on main between 【95†`c36bbb7`】 and 【114†`d87e86e`】. The head merges cleanly into 【114†`d87e86e`】, and on the merged tree `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` is clean for `p3-word` and `p3-word-backend`, with 33, 65 and 19 tests passing.\nL469: With 【91†#2293】's current head (【115†`4dde349`】) merged on top it's still clean and green.\nL470: The byte identity still holds. I reran last round's probes on the merged tree. The composed and lowered forms agree word by word in both segments, give the same commitment and the same postcard bytes, and cross-verify, for all ten shapes from last time: a single instance, dead calls first, in the middle and with an unused spelling, public-only, witness-only, slots with no relations, 1000 empty instances, and the four-call mix. Every byte count matches round c exactly.\nL471: All-dead and no-call statements still refuse with `Shift(DegenerateWiring)` on both forms. The flat fixture gives the same commitment and bytes on 【114†`d87e86e`】 and on this branch at 1, 3, 7 and 16 copies. A key built with `rotr 14` still refuses the `rotr 13` proof.\nL472: The round-c points are closed:\nL473:   * `resolve` now refuses a slot at or past the block width in each segment. My probe gets `UnknownSlot` for `resolve(0, 0, witness(2))`, `resolve(0, 2, witness(7))` and a slot at `u32::MAX`, which no longer panics. When I disable the check, only `a_slot_past_the_block_width_cannot_reach_the_next_instance` fails. The `expect` after it can't fire, because an in-range slot of a laid-out call is below the segment length, which `Composition::new` bounded to `u32::MAX`.\nL474:   * When `compose` stops advancing the relation base, three tests now fail instead of one: `the_relation_base_advances_between_calls`, `the_composed_layout_matches_across_several_calls` and the two-gadget proof.\nL475:   * When `unproved_relations` counts only the flat form, `a_composed_product_relation_leaves_no_key_to_price` fails.\nL476:   * `Composition::new` refuses more than `u32::MAX` instances with `TooManyInstances`, and without that check `an_instance_count_past_the_index_space_is_refused_by_the_layout` fails. `Composition`'s fields are private, so the new `expect` in `compose` is only reachable through `new`.\nL477:   * The opening column, the lane formula and the dead `check_shape` are done.\nL478: Nothing blocks.\nL479: \nL480: Smaller things\nL481:   * The two points from last round's body are still open. The description still says every rejection test names the exact error, but three rejections are still `is_err()` (inline). `interface`, `inputs`, `outputs`, `locals` and the `_mut` forms still slice the caller's buffer without a length check. `locals(&[_; 2], 0, 2)` panics at `component.rs:553` instead of returning an error, and the docs still list only the call and instance errors.\nL498: ` 【117†37a6eb9】 `\nL499: \nL500:     Four rejection assertions settled for \"an error happened\". Each now pins the\nL501:     variant with its payload, measured rather than assumed, and each was checked\nL502:     by removing the code it covers.\nL503: \nL504:     One was misdescribed. Padding the public words of a two-instance proof up to\nL505:     a three-instance statement is rejected on the zerocheck round count, nine\nL506:     against eight, because three instances need a third constraint coordinate.\nL507:     The comment implied the public words were what failed; they are never\nL508:     reached. The comment now says which check fires and why.\nL509: \nL510:     The scalar reference rejection names instance one's linear relation. With\nL511:     the linear family unchecked the same witness is caught by the bitwise family\nL512:     at another position, so the payload distinguishes the two.\nL513: \nL514:     The two transcript rejections are the relation closing check. Dropping the\nL515:     public words from the relation domain separator changes the outcome, which\nL516:     confirms the binding those tests claim to exercise is the cause.\nL517: \nL518:     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nL519: Hide details View details\nL520: \nL521: 【70†tcoratger】 merged commit 【118†`cb69111`】 into Plonky3:main 【119†Sep 22, 2026】\nL522: \nL523: 19 checks passed\nL524: ### Uh oh!\nL525: \nL526: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL527: \nL528: 【120†Sign up for free】 to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? 【1†Sign in to comment】\nL529: \nL5